             width="1400" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
             style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto">
      </div>
      <p class="cst-cap">Left to right: the daily home with the health meter reading at a glance, the purity screen immediately after a logged cigarette, and resources unlocking against completed streaks.</p>
      <p class="cst-caveat"><b>ON THE VISUALS.</b> The screens above are redrawn from the original Figma file so they sit inside this site's grid and type. The source designs, including the onboarding and account flows, are on Behance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ---------- what actually happened ----------
       One number, and it is a small one. On black, but labelled ANECDOTE. -->
  <section class="cst-figures" data-chrome="dark" id="outcomes" aria-labelledby="out-h">
    <div class="cst-shell">
      <h2 id="out-h">One number, and it is a small one.</h2>

      <div class="cst-stats">
        <div class="cst-stat">
          <p class="cst-num">8<span class="sg">+</span></p>
          <p>people reported quitting or cutting down</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cst-stat">
          <p class="cst-num">5<span class="sg">+</span></p>
          <p>years smoking — the group this was designed for</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cst-stat">
          <p class="cst-num">0</p>
          <p>users measured in a shipped product</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="cst-note"><b>ANECDOTE, NOT EVIDENCE.</b> Eight people is a small group, I recruited them myself, they knew I had designed the thing, and I took their word for the outcome without verification. There was no control group and no follow-up past a few weeks. I include the number because it is the only outcome this project has — I'd rather show it with the caveats attached than leave a gap where a result should be.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ---------- honest limits ----------
       What I'd take apart if I picked this up again. -->
  <section class="cst-sec" data-chrome="light" aria-labelledby="lim-h">
    <div class="cst-shell cst-shell--text">
      <h2 class="cst-h2 cst-mark cst-mark--coral" id="lim-h">what I'd take apart<br>if I picked this up again</h2>

      <div class="cst-opts">
        <div class="cst-opt">
          <h3 class="cst-h3">two per cent is a made-up number</h3>
          <p>It's the load-bearing constant in the entire system and I chose it because it felt proportionate. It should be derived from something — cigarettes per day, time since the last one, the shape of the person's own history. As it stands the twentieth cigarette of the day costs the same as the first, which is wrong in both directions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cst-opt">
          <h3 class="cst-h3">the metaphor can turn on you</h3>
          <p>Muddy water representing your lungs is vivid, and vivid cuts both ways. For someone already ashamed of smoking, watching the flask darken could read as the app agreeing with the worst thing they think about themselves. I designed for the encouraging reading and never tested for the punitive one.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cst-opt">
          <h3 class="cst-h3">nobody used it mid-craving</h3>
          <p>Every design decision here is about what happens during a real urge, and I have no observation of a real urge. People walked through the flow calmly, which is the one state it was not built for.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cst-opt">
          <h3 class="cst-h3">gamifying a clinical problem needs more care than I gave it</h3>
          <p>Nicotine dependence has established treatment pathways. Unsmoke touches none of them, has no route to a cessation service or a clinician, and a serious version would need that referral built in rather than treated as out of scope.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cst-opt">
          <h3 class="cst-h3">recovery is asserted, not modelled</h3>
          <p>I show purity rising over clean days, but I never defined the rate or checked it against how lung function actually recovers. If the numbers are invented, a user who learns that stops trusting the display.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ---------- the close ----------
       Happy to be argued with on the two per cent, then the footer signs off. -->
  <section class="cst-sec" data-chrome="light" aria-labelledby="close-h">
    <div class="cst-shell cst-shell--text">
      <h2 class="cst-h2 cst-mark cst-mark--sun" id="close-h">happy to be argued<br>with on the two per cent</h2>
      <p class="cst-p" style="margin-top:.85em">This is the project where I am least sure I got the mechanic right, and the objections to gamifying addiction are good ones. The same question — what does a habit tool show you at the worst moment — carries into the next project, asked of a gentler behaviour.</p>
